WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Researchers melted diamond under crushing pressure to recreate the weather inside ice giants like Neptune. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 178. Looking back over the past week, NASA faced setbacks with a private rescue attempt on the Swift telescope while advancing plans for future servicing demonstrations. ESA and Jaxa prepared for a dual probe arrival at Mercury after an eight-year journey, and several episodes explored new materials

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.960
and launch contracts that could reshape how agencies operate beyond low-earth orbit. Fascinating Frontiers also tracked how living systems might one day manufacture materials in space, building on earlier reports of expanded solar eruption models and vest designs for deep space crews. The experiment recreated pressures and temperatures thought to exist deep inside Neptune and Uranus. At those extremes, carbon separates from other materials and forms diodes

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:14.320
and diamond crystals that sink through the mantle. The team used laser compression on a diamond sample to reach the required conditions for the first time. This approach lets scientists test the long-standing idea that these planets produce literal diamond rain.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:44.300
Follow-up runs will measure how the crystals interact with surrounding ises and fluids. The profile follows Catherine Johnson's journey, beginning with manual computations at Langley Research Center. Her contributions included calculating trajectories for the initial American manned orbital mission. She provided essential support during the Mercury and Apollo programs. Colleagues remembered her commitment to double-checking all figures prior to any submission. This work helped ensure the success of

00:01:44.300 --> 00:02:03.820
early space flights. The article highlights her transition from human computers to key roles in mission planning. It draws from historical records of her time at NASA. On a completely different note, an ongoing attitude control problem on a commercial spacecraft forced NASA and catalyst space to change plans.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:18.980
The link spacecraft will now attempt rendezvous and proximity operations with the Neil Gair Hell Swift Observatory. The goal is to demonstrate technologies needed for future servicing and debris removal missions. No new launch date has been announced.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:38.580
Shifting focus to active experiments already underway, episode 225 of this week in space features Adam Call of KMI, describing experiments on the ISS aimed at gripping and removing debris. The discussion covers mechanical and adhesive methods tested in microgravity.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:03:03.200
Hosts Rod Pyle and Tareek Mollick placed the work in the context of growing low-earth orbit traffic. From current debris removal experiments to a quieter observational milestone from decades past, the supernova in the large Magellanic Cloud had been visible to ground telescopes since 1987. Hubble's sharper optics revealed the expanding ring of material ejected before the explosion.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:21.660
The images allowed direct measurement of the ring's expansion rate. Moving from historic imaging achievements to new ground-based facilities supporting future research, the new 50,000 square foot building will host work on propulsion materials and autonomy for both defense and civil space programs.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:30.260
State officials say the site will support university and industry teams already active in Huntsville. Construction finished ahead of schedule.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:40.200
From institutional growth to personal scale astronomy, a 73 millimeter refractor and stacking software produced the combined image of the moon and Saturn.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:48.840
The photographer added a soft glow around Saturn in post-processing. The result shows both objects at the same focal length without tracking.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:57.980
In another amateur effort, the observer used a 70 millimeter telescope and smartphone, drifting the target across the field for one minute stacks.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:13.660
Software combined hundreds of short exposures into final TIFF files. Targets include the Lagoon Nebula and several star clusters. From backyard imaging to the next predictable celestial event, the August 2026 partial eclipse has passed.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:22.580
The next opportunity for Earth's shadow to reach the moon will occur in 2027. Observers in the Americas and Europe will see the best views.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:32.800
Exact timing depends on the moon's orbital position at the time. Scientists have now melted diamond in the lab to simulate the interiors of Neptune and Uranus.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:38.880
The work shows how carbon can condense into crystals that rain through layers of compressed ice and rock.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.580
The same process may explain why these planets release more heat than they receive from the Sun.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:51.260
Next tests will track how the falling diamonds affect convection and magnetic field generation inside the worlds.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:05:01.660
Neptune's mantle reaches pressures roughly 2 million times higher than the deepest point in Earth's oceans, a force that would crush any familiar material into new forms.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:08.440
Picture writing inward with a carbon atom as surrounding water and ammonia squeeze tighter with every kilometer.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:14.660
At a certain depth, the carbon atoms link into rigid lattices and drop like microscopic hail.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:24.500
Laboratory lasers have now reproduced that exact pressure spike for a few billions of a second, long enough for diamond crystals to nucleate and begin their slow descent.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:35.260
The crystals are thought to reach sizes of centimeters before dissolving again near the core, releasing gravitational energy that helps power the planet's surprisingly strong magnetic field.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:41.800
Yet models still cannot explain why Neptune radiates almost three times more heat than it absorbs from the Sun.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:48.000
The mismatch suggests either an unknown heat source or a flaw in how we picture the rain's efficiency.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:57.300
Future missions carrying seismometers or atmospheric probes could settle the question by measuring the actual rate at which diamonds are forming and falling.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:10.160
Laboratory recreations also raise the possibility that similar carbon behavior occurs inside exoplanets of comparable mass and composition, opening a new window on worlds we can only observe from afar.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:22.320
The timing of crystal formation versus dissolution remains uncertain, leaving open whether the process operates continuously or in episodic bursts tied to internal convection cycles.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:28.700
Space keeps revealing new ways matter behaves under extremes we can only touch briefly on Earth.

